+## JavaScript objects: `.js`, `.JSObject` (both builds)
+
+`js.cls` lets Rexx use JavaScript objects with ordinary messages, in the
+browser build and in the Node command-line build:
+
+```rexx
+say .js~Math~max(3, 7)                       -- 7
+m = .js~Map~new; m~set("a", 1); say m~size   -- 1
+resp = .js~fetch("data.json")~await          -- a Promise, waited for
+fs = .js~require("node:fs")                  -- Node: require, npm modules
+::requires "js.cls"
+```
+
+* `.js` is `globalThis` of the realm: the Web Worker that hosts the run in
+  the browser (fetch, IndexedDB, WebSocket, timers; no DOM), Node's main
+  thread under Node (`.js~require(name)` from the current directory,
+  `.js~import(name)`).  In the browser `.js~page` is the page's `window`,
+  with the DOM (`doc = .js~page~document; p = doc~createElement("p")`);
+  objects of the two realms do not mix (an argument must belong to the
+  realm of the object that gets the message).  Every object, function, array or Promise is a
+  `.JSObject`, by reference; strings, numbers, booleans (1/0) and bigints
+  come back as Rexx strings, null as .nil, undefined as `.js~undefined`
+  (a single object; a property that does not exist reads as it, and
+  `~hasProperty` tells the two apart).
+* ooRexx uppercases message names, so they are resolved case-insensitively
+  along the prototype chain (cached per object; of two matches the one
+  starting in lowercase wins, `window.document` over `window.Document`,
+  else it is an error).  `o~name` gets a property, calling it (with this = o) if it is a
+  function, except constructors (Capitalized, with a prototype:
+  `.js~Map~new`); `o~name(args)` calls a method; `o~name = v` sets an
+  existing property.  Exact: `o["name"]`, `o["name"] = v`,
+  `o~invoke("name", args...)` (`o~invoke(.nil, ...)` calls o itself).  Also
+  `o~new(...)`, `o~await`, `o~typeOf`, `o~className`, `o~instanceOf(c)`,
+  `o~hasProperty(n)`, `o~propertyNames`, `o~makeArray` (`DO x OVER o`: an
+  iterable's values, as `for...of`, so a Map's `[key, value]` pairs; an error
+  for anything else), `o~supplier` (`DO WITH INDEX i ITEM v OVER o`: a Map's
+  keys and values, an iterable's 1..n and values, other objects' own
+  properties), `o~byteString`; names chosen not to hide common JavaScript ones (a Map's
+  get/set/has/keys).  Rexx's own Object methods (send, start, copy, ...)
+  are not passed on: `o~invoke("send", x)`.
+* Rexx strings always go as strings; `.js~num(x)`, `.js~bool(x)`,
+  `.js~true`, `.js~false`, `.js~bigint(x)`, `.js~bytes(x)` (a Uint8Array),
+  `.js~undefined` force a type; an Array goes as an array, a Directory or
+  StringTable as a plain object (copies), .nil as null.
+* A JavaScript exception (or a rejected Promise under `~await`) is SYNTAX
+  98.900 "JavaScript error: TypeError: ..."; what was thrown is
+  `condition("O")~additional[2]`.
+* Several Rexx threads may use it at once; each `~await` blocks only its
+  own thread.
+* **Browser: off unless the page grants it**, realm by realm:
+  `ooRexx.run({..., js: {worker: true, page: true}})`.  A granted program
+  can do everything JavaScript can there: in the worker, fetch with the
+  site's cookies and its IndexedDB; in the page, the DOM and whatever the
+  page's own scripts can.  Grant it to code you trust as you trust your own
+  scripts (the playground does: users run their own code).  Under Node it is
+  always on.
+* Events: JavaScript cannot call into the interpreter, so a callback is a
+  handler whose calls are queued and taken by a Rexx thread:
+  `btn~addEventListener("click", .js~handler(obj, "CLICKED"))`, then
+  `.js~eventLoop` (sends `CLICKED` to `obj` with the call's arguments, until
+  `.js~stopEventLoop`) or `ev = .js~nextEvent([seconds])` + `ev~dispatch`.
+  The handler's function (one per handler and realm, so
+  `removeEventListener` works) returns undefined at once: no answers (no
+  sort comparators), and the browser has finished the dispatch by the time
+  Rexx sees it (`event~currentTarget` is .nil, `event~target` is fine), so
+  what must happen during it is an option: `.js~handler(obj, msg,
+  "preventDefault stopPropagation stopImmediatePropagation")`; option
+  `latest` keeps at most one event of the handler waiting, the newest
+  (mousemove, scroll, resize), and `max n` at most n, dropping the oldest
+  (`max 0` is an error; given both, `latest` wins).  Each event
+  goes to one waiting thread; an error in a handler ends `eventLoop` as any
+  error.  A handler stays alive from its first trip to JavaScript until
+  `handler~release` (its events still waiting are dropped) or the end of
+  the run; in the browser its functions do
+  nothing once the run is over.  Under Node the program's end is the
+  process's end, even with a server listening or a timer pending.
+* Implementation: `web-js.cpp` is the package `js` (`.JSObject`'s methods
+  are native, so errors point at the caller's line); each operation is
+  passed synchronously to the main runtime thread, where `js-bridge.js` (a
+  `--pre-js`) keeps a handle table per realm (one id per object, so `==` is
+  identity; counted, released when the `.JSObject` is garbage collected),
+  resolves names, converts values and may finish later (`~await`).  The
+  realm itself is `js-realm.js`, the same code in the worker, Node and the
+  page; `js-bridge.js` dispatches: requests for the page realm go to the page
+  by `postMessage` (`ooRexx.js` loads `js-realm.js` there on first use and
+  answers), and the interpreter's call is finished when the answer comes
+  back (about 0.3 ms a round trip in Chromium).  A handler's function hands
+  its event to `js_event` (from the page by `postMessage`), which queues it
+  for `.js~nextEvent`.
+
 ## Known limitations / deliberate non-claims
 
 * WebSocket transport (browser, and the MEMFS build under Node): listening
@@ -256,6 +346,8 @@ newImage 300 200; color red; moveTo 10 10; fillRect 100 50; winShow
 * UDP/datagram semantics are qualified only in the Node command-line build.
 * Browser: ADDRESS commands requiring an OS child process fail closed (RC 127);
   ADDRESS JDOR draws (see above).
+* `.JSObject` is in the browser build and the Node command-line build, not
+  in the MEMFS runtime of the CMake build (`bin/`).
 * Maximum recursion depth is set by the JS engine's native stack (internal
   CALL / internal function calls / method calls): Node about 7,100 / 5,400 /
   4,000; browser workers about 900 / 700 / 500 in Chromium, 1,300 / 900 / 700
